---
description: Python code style, typing, toolchain, and project conventions
applies_to:
  - "*.py"
always: true
---

## Set the Python version

Use the latest stable Python release for all repos. Set `requires-python`, `mypy python_version`, `basedpyright pythonVersion`, and `ruff target-version` to that release in `pyproject.toml`.

## Build and manage dependencies

Build with `hatchling`. Manage dependencies with `uv`, and put development dependencies in `[dependency-groups] dev`. Do not put development dependencies in `[project.optional-dependencies]`.

Every Model Context Protocol (MCP) server repo needs an `install.sh` that finishes setup in one run. Keep `README.md` current. Include prerequisites, setup, and development instructions in that file.

## Annotate types

Fully type every function, method, parameter, and return value. Write a generic with its type parameters, as in `dict[str, float]`, `list[CsvRow]`, or `Callable[[str], bool]`.

Do not use `Any` or `object` as a type annotation. Use `JsonValue`, a concrete union, or a Pydantic model.

Narrow a type at a boundary with `isinstance`, `TypeGuard`, or Pydantic `model_validate`. Use the concrete type after that check.

## Model structured data

Use Pydantic `BaseModel` for all structured data.

When external API JSON uses camelCase keys, set `Field(alias="camelCase")` and `model_config = ConfigDict(populate_by_name=True)`. Use snake_case for each Python attribute. Read each value as an attribute. Serialize with `model_dump(by_alias=True)`.

## Follow required practices

- Start every module with `from __future__ import annotations`.
- Set the line length to 90. `ruff format` wraps lines.
- Write an `if` / `else` block. Do not write a ternary expression.
- Write an explicit loop when a comprehension has more than one clause. Keep a single-clause comprehension when it fits on one line.
- Write a full-word variable, such as `attempt_count` or `error_message`. Use a single letter only for a numeric loop index (`i`, `j`, or `k`).
- Extract each literal value into a named constant.
- When a linter reports a rule, fix or restructure the code.

## Write readable code

Break a complex expression into intermediate variables. Use a descriptive label for each variable. Extract a repeated pattern into a function or utility.

## Run the toolchain

Run `make check` before committing.

- `mypy` enforces full typing. The required settings are `strict`, `disallow_any_explicit`, `disallow_any_generics`, and `disallow_subclassing_any`.
- Run `basedpyright` in strict mode. Do not use `pyright`. `basedpyright` applies stricter defaults.
- `ruff check` enforces import order, naming, and code patterns.
- Enable pyflakes (`F`), pycodestyle (`E` and `W`), isort, pep8-naming (`N`), pyupgrade (`UP`), flake8-annotations (`ANN`), flake8-bugbear (`B`), flake8-builtins (`A`), flake8-comprehensions (`C4`), flake8-return (`RET`), flake8-simplify (`SIM`), flake8-type-checking (`TC`), flake8-pyi (`PYI`), flake8-pytest-style (`PT`), and the Ruff set (`RUF`).
- Ignore `TC001`, `TC003`, `RET504`, `SIM108`, and `N815`.
- `ruff format` applies all whitespace.
- `ban-object` (`scripts/ban_object.py`) rejects a bare `object` in a type annotation.
- `make fmt` runs `ruff format` and `ruff check --fix`.
